Requested 2026-08-30 by Max. Extension repo: ../lsp-openplanet-ext/
(package `packages/vscode-ext`). Enhancement, not a regression.

## Gap
The only way to manage preprocessor defines today is to hand-edit the
`openplanetLsp.defines` array in settings JSON. Verified on master:
`packages/vscode-ext/package.json` contributes exactly ONE command
(`openplanetLsp.restart` / "Restart Server"). There is no command-palette
surface for defines at all.

The setting itself (`contributes.configuration.properties`):

    "openplanetLsp.defines": { "type": "array", "items": {"type":"string"}, "default": [] }

with the documented semantics: entries are ADDED to the defines derived from
the game target, the signature level and `info.toml` `[script].defines`; an
entry prefixed `-` or `!` turns a define OFF (e.g. `-LOGS`). Read in
`packages/vscode-ext/src/extension.ts:24` and forwarded to the server as
`defines` in `packages/vscode-ext/src/settings.ts:130`.

So the array is really a tri-state edit list over a derived base set — exactly
the kind of thing raw JSON is worst at, because the user cannot see the base
set they are editing against.

## Ask
An easy way to add / remove / change / disable defines from the VS Code UI,
via the command palette.

## Design decision (coordinator, 2026-08-30)

Ship a **QuickPick toggle list** as the primary surface, plus thin
add/remove commands. Rationale: the value of this feature is seeing the
RESOLVED define set (derived + manifest + overrides) in one list with its
current state, which a plain input box cannot give.

Commands to contribute:
- `openplanetLsp.defines.manage` — "Openplanet: Manage Defines". Opens a
  multi-select QuickPick listing the resolved define set. Each row shows the
  define name, its state (on/off) and its ORIGIN (target / signature level /
  info.toml / user setting), origin shown as QuickPick `description` and
  greyed. Toggling a row writes the minimal `-NAME` / `NAME` entry into
  `openplanetLsp.defines`; a row returned to its derived state has its
  override REMOVED rather than a redundant entry added.
- `openplanetLsp.defines.add` — "Openplanet: Add Define". Input box, validates
  the name, appends to the array.
- `openplanetLsp.defines.remove` — "Openplanet: Remove Define". QuickPick over
  the user's OWN override entries only, removes the selected ones.
- `openplanetLsp.defines.clearOverrides` — "Openplanet: Clear Define
  Overrides". Empties the array back to the derived set, with a confirm.

Scope target: workspace settings by default, with a scope prompt (Workspace /
User) when a workspace is open — a define override is almost always
project-specific, so Workspace is the right default.

## Open dependency — resolved-define introspection

The extension cannot render origins or the resolved set without asking the
server for it. Check whether such a request already exists before adding one:
grep the server for an existing custom LSP request exposing config/defines
(the `openplanet-lsp` CLI already has `typedb status`-style introspection, so
there may be a config equivalent). If none exists, add a custom request
(suggested: `openplanet-lsp/resolvedDefines`) returning, per define,
`{ name, active, origin }`, and version it.

If that server-side request turns out to be nontrivial, ship the add/remove/
clear commands FIRST against the raw array (they need no server support at
all) and file the origin-annotated manage view as a follow-up — do not block
the whole feature on introspection.

## Interaction with B017 / B019 / B024
- B024 already made config layers ACCUMULATE `defines` into
  `LspConfig::define_overrides`, applied after target derivation and the
  manifest, honouring `-NAME` / `!NAME` removes. The manage view must reflect
  that ordering, not re-derive its own.
- B019 stops defining `DEPENDENCY_<ID>` for UNRESOLVED dependencies. Those
  should still be listed in the manage view (greyed, origin "dependency —
  not installed") and be toggleable ON, since that is the documented way to
  check the branch a user with the dep would compile. Coordinate with B019's
  decision that `define_matrix` can toggle such a define explicitly.
- B017 changes config precedence; the "origin" label must name the winning
  layer under the FINAL ladder, not the current inverted one. So land this
  after B017/B019 to avoid encoding the old precedence into the UI.

## Test plan (TDD, red first)
Tests live in the extension repo (`vitest.config.ts` at its root; existing
tests cover `settings.ts`). Keep the define-edit logic in a PURE module
(e.g. `src/defines.ts`) so it is testable without the vscode API — mirror how
`settings.ts` is already structured and tested.

Red (pure logic, no vscode):
- `toggle_off_adds_minus_entry`: define derived-on, toggled off -> array gains
  `-NAME`.
- `toggle_back_on_removes_override`: same define toggled on again -> array
  returns to its ORIGINAL contents (no redundant `NAME` entry left behind).
- `toggle_on_for_derived_off_adds_bare_entry`: define not in the derived set,
  toggled on -> array gains `NAME`.

Variants (per CLAUDE.md, generalize):
- both negation prefixes `-NAME` and `!NAME` round-trip identically
- a define already present as BOTH `NAME` and `-NAME` in the array
  (contradictory user input) -> deterministic resolution, and the UI shows the
  effective state
- duplicate entries collapse rather than accumulate
- name validation: empty, whitespace-only, leading digit, lowercase, dots,
  spaces, symbols, very long name; reject or normalise consistently with what
  the server accepts (see `dependency_define_suffix` in src/config.rs and the
  B019 lane's findings on ID -> define conversion)
- case handling: `dev` vs `DEV` — decide whether the UI upcases, and lock it
- clearing overrides when the array is already empty -> no-op, no error
- workspace vs user scope: writing to one does not clobber the other
- no workspace open -> scope prompt is skipped, writes to User
- server restart / config push is triggered exactly once per edit batch, not
  per toggled row

## Related
Server side: src/config.rs (define ladder, `define_overrides`, `apply_manifest`).
Backlog: B017 (precedence), B019 (DEPENDENCY_ defines), B024 (define accumulation).